WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.560
An Out-of-place artifact is any historical&nbsp;
object that seems, at a glance, to go against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.240
our understanding of historical chronology. Some&nbsp;
artifacts appear "too advanced" for the technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.080
of the period they were created, and seem like&nbsp;
they’ve fallen out of time. The exciting mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.760
of these artifacts has led to a range of wild&nbsp;
speculation, involving everything from aliens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.800
time travel. As much fun as it is to think someone&nbsp;
with a flux-capacitor popped by and accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.760
left a cell phone in ancient Rome, most of&nbsp;
these objects have reasonable explanations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.080
But the real historical context behind these&nbsp;
Out-of-place artifacts and the ancient cultures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.200
which produced them can be just as interesting&nbsp;
as speculative theories. So, let’s take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.760
seven of the most thought-provoking, and uncover&nbsp;
how such astounding artifacts were created:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.720
The Antikythera Mechanism is an ancient Greek&nbsp;
object which has amazed archeologists, historians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.080
and classicists for decades. The artifact consists&nbsp;
of various incomplete bronze gear fragments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.680
which once formed a kind of primitive machine,&nbsp;
sometimes described as the world’s first analogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.320
computer. Most archeologists believe Greek&nbsp;
Scientists constructed the mechanism around 87 BC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:17.200
it was discovered in a shipwreck off the coast of&nbsp;
Antikythera island. The technological prowess on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.640
display wouldn’t be matched until over a thousand&nbsp;
years later, hence why it seems so out of place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.600
In truth, however, the Greeks around this period&nbsp;
were just that proficient with complex gear-work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.720
with ancient literature describing the&nbsp;
construction of similar devices. But, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.560
did this early machine do? Based on recreations,&nbsp;
archeologists think the mechanism was a sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.160
astronomical clock, able to predict the position&nbsp;
of the moon and even eclipses decades in advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.920
likely for the purpose of astrology and cultural&nbsp;
festivals. Other devices like astrolabes were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.280
in later centuries to make similar calculations,&nbsp;
but none were at the same level as the Antikythera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.680
Mechanism for quite a while, making the&nbsp;
invention quite impressive — even if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:06.240
isn’t ‘out of place.’ Up next we have the Roman&nbsp;
dodecahedron, small hollow objects made in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.000
2nd to 4th centuries CE, which look quite bizarre&nbsp;
at a glance. The twelve-sided shapes are made of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.560
copper, and were spread all over the roman&nbsp;
empire, with archeologists discovering over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.760
a hundred since the 18th century. Funnily enough,&nbsp;
as far as we’ve been able to uncover, the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.600
Romans never recorded what the purpose of these&nbsp;
dodecahedrons were. While the lack of explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.720
lends the objects a certain mysterious power,&nbsp;
truthfully, it’s more likely the Romans never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.920
recorded their purpose because they were ordinary&nbsp;
enough everybody already knew what they were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.880
Archeologists have put forth various theories&nbsp;
regarding their usage, including dice, coin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.880
measuring… and knitting aides but in this case,&nbsp;
the right answer is probably the most mundane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.800
Trace amounts of wax have been found inside some&nbsp;
of objects, suggesting the dodecahedrons were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.840
as candle holders. The Iron pillar of Delhi is a&nbsp;
metal structure now located in Delhi, India dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:09.600
back to 4th or 5th century CE. At 7.2 meters&nbsp;
or roughly 24 feet high, and over three tons or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.600
6,000 pounds in weight, it’s an incredible feat&nbsp;
of engineering. Any normal iron pillar would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.600
corroded away long ago, yet the pillar of Delhi&nbsp;
is famous for its astonishing resistance to rust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.640
despite being over a thousand years old. Indeed,&nbsp;
some have claimed that pillar is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.240
rust-free, and therefore must be the result of&nbsp;
something beyond the realm of science. Any actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.240
photos of the pillar make it clear this isn’t the&nbsp;
case — there unmistakably is rust on the pillar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.920
even if it’s in unbelievable condition. The secret&nbsp;
to the pillar’s longevity lies in the skill of 4th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.640
century Indian metallurgists, who constructed&nbsp;
the iron pillar through a complex process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.800
involving rust resistant compounds to ensure its&nbsp;
survival. It’s an inspiring feat of engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.320
even if it’s not something historians somehow&nbsp;
‘can’t explain.’ The Stone Spheres of Costa Rica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:04.160
are a collection of over 300 circular rocks with&nbsp;
mysterious origins. The objects are attributed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.720
the Diquís culture, an indigenous civilization of&nbsp;
Costa Rica which flourished from 700 to 1530 CE.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.760
The spheres come in all sizes, and have a certain&nbsp;
mystery to them compounded by the fact that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.480
exact significance remains shrouded in uncertainty&nbsp;
— with the most popular theory proposing they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.400
placed in lines before the houses of rulers.&nbsp;
Regardless of their exact meaning, some have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.280
suggested the spheres’ size and impressive&nbsp;
geometric roundness were beyond the capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.160
of the Diquís culture. Frankly, this isn’t&nbsp;
giving the Diquís enough credit —the civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.280
was highly advanced, producing other forms of&nbsp;
impressive sculpture like the metal effigy you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.920
currently. Whatever the cultural purpose behind&nbsp;
the spheres, there’s no reason to suspect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.400
Diquís society didn’t produce them. The Maine&nbsp;
penny is a Norwegian silver coin dating back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.600
the Viking age. The coin itself is ordinary enough&nbsp;
— but what makes it an Out-of-place artifact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.760
is the fact it was discovered in, well, in the&nbsp;
state of Maine in the United States. The coin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.800
existence has been suggested as evidence a group&nbsp;
of Vikings made it all the way to the shores of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:12.560
modern day North America during their travels.&nbsp;
When the coin was first discovered in 1957 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.920
idea Vikings ever crossed the Atlantic Ocean was&nbsp;
pretty new, but today, it’s a much more widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.360
accepted theory thanks to other forms of evidence.&nbsp;
While the coin itself does appear to be an actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.440
Viking coin, here’s still some debate as to&nbsp;
whether it might have be brought over by a later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.049
traveler. There’s still some debate regarding the&nbsp;
coin’s authenticity, but it matches other Viking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.049 --> 00:05:32.320
coins minted during the period on the other side&nbsp;
of the ocean. Indeed, this is the one artifact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.624
this list where the far-out explanation is likely&nbsp;
the correct one — although the coin probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.624 --> 00:05:40.880
started in Canada closer to Greenland and made&nbsp;
its way down through trade. Dogū are small statues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.840
from Jōmon period of Japan with a distinctive&nbsp;
appearance. To modern observers, the statues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:51.360
almost resemble a person wearing a spacesuit.&nbsp;
So, did an alien or a human time traveler decked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.080
out in anachronistic gear accidentally stumble&nbsp;
around ancient Japan long enough to get statues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.280
made in their likeness? Mmmaybe... but it’s&nbsp;
far more likely the resemblance to any modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.880
clothing is coincidental. If these statues&nbsp;
are considered in context with other versions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.240
you can see most Dogū look decidedly far off from&nbsp;
the distinctive space-suit like appearance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.240
more famous figurines. Most Dogū effigies from&nbsp;
the surrounding period actually place emphasis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.080
the wide hips more than anything else, with some&nbsp;
lacking heads altogether. The exaggerated hips and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.560
abdomens of these statues have led historians&nbsp;
to theorize the they. were in fact associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.800
with fertility. Maybe not as exciting as time&nbsp;
travel, but still pretty interesting nonetheless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.200
Concluding our list, we have the Eltanin Antenna,&nbsp;
a mysterious object photographed on the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:47.760
floor in the 1960s. Antarctic research ship USNS&nbsp;
Eltanin is the vessel which captured the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.960
antenna like-artifact on camera. The ship was&nbsp;
traveling west of Cape Horn in South America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:58.960
when crew-members spotted the object at a&nbsp;
strikingly deep depth of over 3,900 meters or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.867
12,800 ft below the ocean’s surface — in a region&nbsp;
close to total darkness. So, what was this alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.867 --> 00:07:12.400
looking object so far below sea level? Probably&nbsp;
a type of sea sponge. Chondrocladia concrescens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.920
is a species of carnivorous sponge with unusual&nbsp;
appendages which vaguely resembles an antenna.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.480
A pretty wild organism, even if it’s not quite&nbsp;
as astounding as some deep-sea radio dish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.680
And that’s the end of our analysis of seven&nbsp;
interesting Out-of-place artifacts and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:37.040
explanations behind them. I hope you had fun&nbsp;
and perhaps learned something along the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.240
If you did, please consider&nbsp;
subscribing to Curious Archive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:57.840
and leaving this video a like. Thank you so much&nbsp;
for watching, and I’ll see you in the next video.

